returns a relative PATH entry.  run_finish runs the finish script with cwd set
to the scratch repository, where that entry names nothing: the real mv runs and
the script publishes.  The test asserts the shim fires; at this commit it fails
with FINISH DONE and rc 0, exactly as the reviewer reproduced.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

3b76d32 exp_10 round 10: the PATH shims resolve $TMPDIR before inspecting it (green)
Round-9 review, finding 1 (blocking).  os.path.abspath() runs before the
os.pathsep / whitespace checks, so a relative $TMPDIR becomes an absolute base
and mkdtemp returns a PATH entry that still names the shim directory after
run_finish changes cwd.  The order matters: an absolute form carrying a colon
must still fall back to /tmp.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

eeba144 exp_10 round 10: a regression test for the queue's fake nvidia-smi (red)
Round-9 review, finding 2.  run_script prepends <root>/fakebin to PATH and the
shell splits PATH on os.pathsep, so under a root containing a colon the fake is
skipped and the machine's real nvidia-smi answers the queue's resource guard --
silently, since the guard then measures whatever the host GPU has free.  The
fake now records its calls next to itself so a test can prove it replied; at
this commit the colon regression fails with no marker file and the host's
figure in the queue log, while the same fake writes the marker from a root
without a colon.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

45e5c16 exp_10 round 10: one rule for every PATH stand-in, shared by both fixtures (green)
Round-9 review, finding 2.  fx.safe_bin_dir() is the placement rule the shim
directory already used -- $TMPDIR made absolute first, /tmp when the absolute
form carries os.pathsep or whitespace, mkdtemp under it, loud refusal rather
than a silently skipped stand-in -- and make_queue_repo now builds its fake
nvidia-smi there instead of <root>/fakebin, with the queue fixture removing it.
shim_bin keeps its finalizer and drops its own copy of the rule.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

